      "writtenOn": "2026-03-16T17:52:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There\u0027s some repeated logic here that we can simplify *and* make more readable with table-based testing\nhttp://go/go-style/decisions#table-driven-tests\nUnlike that link, which has a test struct and then makes an array of structs, and then has a for loop go through them all, I prefer a different approach that I think is easier to read and easier to debug. [example](https://github.com/google/skia/blob/df5f12318313254224cb284b56c979bf55ba2dd7/tests/QuadRootsTest.cpp#L92-L114)\n\n```\n// Psuedocode using a lambda which captures reporter and pixmap for less boilerplate\nauto test \u003d [](std::string name, SkPoint pt, SkColor expected) {\n    skiatest::ReporterContext subtest(reporter, name);\n    SkColor actual \u003d pixmap.getColor(pt.x(), pt.y());\n    REPORTER_ASSERT(reporter, actual \u003d\u003d expected, \"%x !\u003d %x\", actual, expected);  // I used hex to make it more clear what the channels are if they differ\n};\n\ntest(\"above north line\", {5.0, 4.0}, SK_ColorWHITE);\ntest(\"on north line\", {5.0, 5.0}, SK_ColorBLACK);\ntest(\"below north line\", {5.0, 6.0}, SK_ColorWHITE);\n\n... repeat 3x for other borders and maybe for the corners.\n```",
      "revId": "e3a7fcd93bfbe4d915cca90b2302dcddbbf3c78f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
